{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>וַיִּסַּע מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים וגו׳.</b> This verse shines a path [to understanding] in light of what we explained regarding the verse (Exodus 13:21) ״וַה׳ הוֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיהֶם״ — that three clouds served Israel: one before them to scout out a place for them, and a second to shield them from the heat of the sun, and a third to illuminate for them at night. And the verse informs us of what HaShem did that night: <b>וַיִּסַּע מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים</b> — that is, the one going before them, and it is the first cloud mentioned in the section of Devarim (Deuteronomy 1:33), where it says ״הַהֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיכֶם״ — it journeyed and went behind them. And it further says that the cloud serving in front of them, whose time was all day, when its time came to journey at night — and it is the one mentioned in the section of Devarim, where it is written (ibid.) ״וּבֶעָנָן יוֹמָם״ — it too journeyed and stood behind them. You thus find that two clouds that had been before them journeyed behind them, and the reason for the second one was so that it would grow dark before them, without Israel knowing the location of their stations. And this is what the verse means by <b>וְהָיָה הֶעָנָן וְהַחֹשֶׁךְ וַיָּאֶר אֶת הַלָּיְלָה</b> — meaning that for both of them it was necessary to bring darkness in front of them, lest the camp of Israel be known [to the Egyptians], for there was light in it at night, and the light would reveal their location to them. Therefore darkness covered their front, and darkness alone did not suffice, for the light of Israel in their encampments would gleam through. Therefore HaShem made two — the cloud and the darkness."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:59.298070368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}